  "notes": [
    "Maven manual/total = 43855/75342 = 58.2%; same-day manual = 19792 (45.1% of manual); <=1-day manual = 20356 (46.4% of manual).",
    "The main propagation Cox (analyze_cross_ecosystem.py) already restricts to propagation_days>0, so same-day events never entered the survival estimates; the re-fit here additionally removes the day==1 manual events.",
    "Measured bot rates: npm 8.0% / PyPI 16.2% among classified (7.1% / 12.4% on the full sampled denominator); Maven unclassifiable (no SCM URLs).",
    "Direction of bias: if Maven 'manual' is partly bots, the Maven manual rate is OVERSTATED. In the extreme worst case (every <=1-day manual event is a bot) Maven's human-driven manual rate falls to 31.2%, which remains an order of magnitude above npm (3.6%) but is NO LONGER guaranteed to exceed PyPI (34.5%); the qualitative contrast with npm and the three-orders-of-magnitude AUTO-propagation gap (an auto-, not manual-, quantity) are untouched.",
    "Robustness: health-agnostic bot updates attenuate health hazard ratios toward 1 (making TOST EASIER to pass), so contamination cannot manufacture the decoupling. Re-fitting per-ecosystem TOST after excluding <=1-day manual events leaves the decoupling intact (Maven log_contributors HR moves 0.886->0.874, still equivalent only at delta=0.30; npm/PyPI remain equivalent at small margins) - the borderline Maven contributor result is NOT an artifact of bot contamination."
  ]
}